    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away. Let's focus on the physics.\n\nIn an AC circuit, the capacitive reactance (Xc) is inversely proportional to the frequency (f) of the circuit. This means that as the frequency increases, the capacitive reactance decreases. Conversely, as the frequency decreases, the capacitive reactance increases.\n\nTo see why, imagine a capacitor as a barrier to the flow of current. When the frequency is high, the capacitor can't keep up with the rapid changes in current, so it acts more like a resistor. As the frequency decreases, the capacitor has more time to charge and discharge, making it more effective at opposing the current. This is why capacitive reactance is inversely proportional to frequency.\n\nFor example, if you double the frequency of an AC circuit, the capacitive reactance will halve. This is why capacitors are often used in high-frequency applications, such as radio circuits, where their reactance is minimized.",
